Kind Makes Example <code>flags</code> TRUE if the pattern appears, and otherwise FALSE. <code>opinion,criminal_firearm,flags,[Pp]ossession of (a )?[Ff]irearm</code> <code>counts</code> The number of times the pattern appears. <code>opinion,habeas_mentions,counts,habeas</code> <code>matches</code> Every match, separated by the <code>separator</code>. <code>opinion,citations_case,matches,\d+ (?:F\.(?:[23]d\|4th)\|U\.S\.) \d+,,,;</code> <code>match</code> The first match. A later rule can search it as a new section of text. <code>header,counsel,match,
COUNSEL\:.*?(?=

)</code> <code>category</code> The rule's <code>value</code> if the pattern appears. The first rule for a name that matches is used. <code>court,court_num,category,COURT OF APPEALS FOR THE FIRST CIRCUIT,1</code> (as made from <code>courts.csv</code>) <code>categories</code> The <code>value</code> of every rule for the name whose pattern appears, once each, in the order of the rules and separated by the <code>separator</code> (such as "Internal Revenue Service; United States Tax Court"). A value cannot have the separator. <code>opinion,agencies,categories,NLRB (?&lt;![\w-]NLRB),National Labor Relations Board</code> <code>names</code> The names in the text, found by splitting it wherever the pattern matches, and separated by the <code>separator</code>. The pattern should match what comes between names: titles, filler words, and punctuation. <code>judges,panel_judges,names, (?:BEFORE\|CIRCUIT\|JUDGES?\|AND) \|[,;:],,,;</code> <code>split</code> New sections, named in <code>name</code>: the text before and after the first match. <code>party,"party1, party2",split,\Wv\.\W</code> <code>replace</code> The text with every match replaced by the <code>value</code> (a blank <code>value</code> removes the matches). Without a <code>name</code>, it changes the section for the rules below it. <code>party1,,replace, ET AL </code> <p><code>amos</code> has more kinds (such as <code>fields</code>, which makes a column for each labeled part of a text); see its <code>amos.parsers</code> module.</p>
